# Demo requests for the Notification Service.
# Start the app first (docker compose up -d, then run the "http" profile), then click "Send request" above a request.
# Requests are separated by ###. Named requests (# @name) can be referenced by later ones, e.g. {{sendSms.response.body.$.id}}.

@host = http://localhost:5080

# Idempotency keys are stored permanently: change this value to start the idempotency demo (requests 3-5) from scratch.
@idempotencyKey = order-42-shipped-1

### 1. Send an SMS -> 202 Accepted with a Location header
# @name sendSms
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Sms",
  "recipient": "+37060012345",
  "body": "Your verification code is 123456"
}

### 2. Get the SMS: status and delivery attempts (send it a few times; Twilio fails ~30% in Development, then Vonage delivers)
GET {{host}}/notifications/{{sendSms.response.body.$.id}}

### 3. Send an email with an idempotency key -> 202 Accepted (the email appears in Mailpit: http://localhost:8025)
# @name sendEmail
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Email",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "subject": "Your order has shipped",
  "body": "Your order #42 will arrive tomorrow."
}

### 4. Repeat exactly the same request -> 200 OK with the same id, nothing new is created
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Email",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "subject": "Your order has shipped",
  "body": "Your order #42 will arrive tomorrow."
}

### 5. Same idempotency key, different body -> 409 Conflict
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Email",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "subject": "Your order has shipped",
  "body": "A different message."
}

### 6. Get the email
GET {{host}}/notifications/{{sendEmail.response.body.$.id}}

### 7. Invalid phone number -> 400 problem details from the domain
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Sms",
  "recipient": "12345",
  "body": "Hello"
}

### 8. Email without a subject -> 400
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Email",
  "recipient": "jane@example.com",
  "body": "Hello"
}

### 9. Missing channel -> 400 validation problem
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "recipient": "+37060012345",
  "body": "Hello"
}

### 10. Unknown channel -> 400 naming the field, without echoing the value
POST {{host}}/notifications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerId": "customer-1",
  "channel": "Pigeon",
  "recipient": "+37060012345",
  "body": "Hello"
}

### 11. Unknown notification -> 404
GET {{host}}/notifications/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001

### 12. Liveness: the process is up
GET {{host}}/health/live

### 13. Readiness: the database is reachable
GET {{host}}/health/ready
